From da34f4511a108e8c6423a11524327602b282394d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MasherDon <62326358+MasherDon@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:27:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 27/91] moe-stream : stage dispatcher - a guess's copy waits
 until the copy engine has no demand left, at most 2 staged slabs in flight

Every copy goes into one in-order stream, and a staged slab (a guess for a later call: the oracle's VRAM_AHEAD, A''s
PRED_VRAM) used to enter it the moment its read finished, ahead of the current call's demand copies. Now the read
still starts at once, but the worker leaves the copy in stage_copies with its slab pinned in L2. A dispatcher thread
enqueues one only when the latest demand copy has landed (its event) and fewer than N staged copies are in flight (a
ring of N events), so a demand copy waits behind at most N staged slabs. A call that finds a staged slot still
loading takes its queued copies at once, as demand, and parts still being read follow (slot_wanted). A slot is
RESIDENT once all its copies are enqueued, as with the async uploads. On detach (or a second context) the deferred
copies are made synchronously. LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_STAGE_INFLIGHT=N, default 2, 0 = off; it needs the per-slot fences
and acts only where something stages.

SiegeLadder runs.md 4.40, main mode: the oracle at recall 1.0 17.27 -> 17.77 / 17.97 tok/s (18.00 with the default),
with A''s false guesses 13.5 -> 14.49 / 14.38, A' top 3 12.16 -> 12.52; the base unchanged (12.19, nothing to
dispatch); text = trace in every run; A' top-3 staging with and without it gives the same KL (0.000000, max 5.2e-05).
The rejected stage gate (holding the staged reads too) lost 5-10%. False guesses stay costly through bus volume: 457
copies per token instead of 312, the bus 72-77% busy.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
